 "path": "memory/continual-arc-task-caaf6112.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC task-caaf6112\n\nSolved this task from 5 demonstrations (3 wrong submissions along the way; avoid repeating errors). General rule: input contains two same-height raster panels separated by a solid-color divider (often vertical or horizontal). One panel has a sparse random foreground pattern and the other is a mostly filled inverse/background pattern; output selects the sparse panel, crops away the divider and other panel, changes its foreground color to 2, and preserves its zero background. Use the panel consistent with demos; the successful test output was 27x14, taking the left panel (the 14 columns before the vertical 6 divider), mapping its 9 cells to 2 and keeping zeros. Critical refinement: output is the *overlap/intersection* mask of the sparse left foreground and matching-color/foreground positions in the opposing panel, not all sparse foreground pixels. On this test, output each cell as 2 iff left cell is 9 AND corresponding right-side cell (starting immediately after divider) is 7; otherwise 0. This exact rule produced the correct grid. Keep original row count and left width, discard separator and right panel. Demos illustrate panels separated vertically or horizontally, pattern transfer/intersection and recoloring foreground to 2, zeros unchanged."
